Ladybird now hides an atomic inline that text-overflow has no room for
as a whole, places the ellipsis right after the content before it, and
keeps the first content on a line, clipped. Blink’s LineTruncator::
HideChild() hides atomic inlines the same way, and Gecko’s TextOverflow
treats them as units through IsAtomicElement(). Checked in Chrome
158.0.8127.37, Firefox 151.0, and WebKit 26.5: Chrome draws the test and
its reference identically, and so does Firefox, but for antialiasing.
WebKit differs on one case only, a hidden box with a start margin:
It puts the ellipsis after that margin, where Chrome, Firefox, and
Ladybird put it after the text.
